"H3605":"all","H1952":"substance","H3368":"precious","H5273":"pleasant","H1397":"great man","H2450":"wise","H5797":"strength","H553":"to strengthen","H3581":"reptile","H8458":"counsel","H6213":"do","H4421":"battle","H8668":"salvation","H7230":"abundance","H3289":"to advise","H7311":"to exalt","H191":"fool(ish)","H2454":"wisdom","H8179":"gate","H3808":"not","H6605":"to open","H6310":"lip","H2803":"design","H7489":"be evil","H1167":"master","H4209":"plot","H7121":"call to","H2154":"wickedness","H200":"folly","H2403":"sin","H8441":"abomination","H120":"man","H3887":"to mock","H7503":"to slacken","H3117":"day","H6869":"dearth","H6862":"narrow","H5337":"to rescue","H3947":"take","H4194":"death","H4131":"to shake","H2027":"slaughter","H518":"if","H2820":"to withhold","H559":"to say","H2005":"look!","H3045":"to know","H2088":"this","H8505":"to measure","H3826":"heart","H1931":"he/she/it","H995":"to understand","H5341":"to watch","H5315":"soul","H7725":"return","H6467":"work","H398":"to eat","H1121":"child","H1706":"honey","H2896":"pleasant","H5317":"honey","H4966":"sweet","H5921":"upon","H2441":"palate","H3651":"right","H4672":"to find","H3426":"there","H319":"end","H8615":"cord","H3772":"cut","H693":"to ambush","H7563":"wicked","H5116":"pasture","H6662":"righteous","H7703":"to ruin","H7258":"rest","H7651":"seven","H5307":"fall","H6965":"(Leb)-kamai","H3782":"to stumble","H341":"enemy","H8055":"to rejoice","H1523":"to rejoice","H6435":"lest","H7200":"see","H3068":"LORD","H5869":"eye","H639":"anger","H2734":"to be incensed","H5216":"lamp","H1846":"to put out","H3372":"to fear","H853":"[Obj.]","H4428":"king","H5973":"with","H8138":"to change","H6148":"to pledge","H6597":"suddenly","H343":"calamity","H6365":"disaster","H8147":"two","H4310":"who?","H1571":"also","H428":"these","H5234":"to recognize","H6440":"before","H4941":"judgement","H1077":"not","H859":"you(m.s.)","H5344":"to pierce","H5971":"people","H2194":"be indignant","H3816":"people","H3198":"to rebuke","H5276":"be pleasant","H935":"come","H1293":"blessing","H5401":"to kiss","H1697":"word","H5228":"straightforward","H2351":"outside","H4399":"work","H6257":"be ready","H7704":"Sirion","H310":"after","H5707":"witness","H2600":"for nothing","H7453":"neighbor","H6601":"to open wide","H834":"which","H6102":"sluggish","H5674":"to pass","H3754":"vineyard","H2638":"lacking","H2009":"behold","H5927":"rise","H7063":"thorn","H3680":"to cover","H2738":"nettle","H1444":"wall","H68":"stone","H2040":"to overthrow","H2372":"to see","H595":"I","H7896":"make","H4148":"discipline","H4592":"little","H8142":"sleep","H8572":"slumber","H2264":"folding","H3027":"hand","H7901":"lay down","H1980":"went","H7389":"poverty","H4270":"need","H4043":"shield"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Like. Be not allured by their prosperity to imitate them, Psalm xxxvi. 1."],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Wisdom and virtue, and not by injustice can the house be established."],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Valiant, as well as a good economist, ver. 4. (Calmet)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Counsels. “Consult many what ought to be done, but only a few of the most faithful, or rather thyself alone, what thou art about to do.” (Veget. iii. 9., and 27.)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","High. Thus the fool excuses himself. But wisdom condescends to our weakness, if we be truly in earnest, Deuteronomy xxxii. 12. Mouth. To defend himself, or to give advice. (Calmet)"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Of a fool. In as much as he is wicked. Though he may have some pious thoughts, he attends not to them. (Haydock) — He thinks how he may commit evil, and renders himself hateful. (Calmet) — Hebrew, “a wicked thought is the sin of folly.” Septuagint, “the fool dieth in sins.” — Detractor. Hebrew, “scoffer.” (Haydock)"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Diminished. This is the sad consequences of too much dejection, Ephesians iv. 19. Despairing, they abandon themselves to impurities. (Haydock)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Deliver. The Jews often put people to death without any formal trial, pretending zeal, as they did St. Stephen, &c. Our Saviour rescued the adulteress from such a situation, as Daniel had done Susanna. Yet this text may regard poor debtors, Psalm lxxi. 4. (Calmet) — Christian bishops used all their influence to preserve the lives of those who did not deserve death. (St. Ambrose in Psalm cxviii. Ser. viii. Off. i. 36., and ep. xxv., and xxvi.)"],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","I have. Hebrew, “behold, we know not this man.” (Pagnin) (Haydock) — He is a stranger. But all mankind are brethren, and have a charge to assist one another, even though they be enemies, Ecclesiasticus xvii. 12., and Exodus xxiii. 4. (Calmet) — Keeper. Hebrew notser, “preserver.” As thou hast received many good things from God, shew mercy to thy neighbour."],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Honey. Of wisdom, which is most delicious. (Menochius)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Thou shalt. Hebrew, “yea, it is the last.” (Montanus) — “Then there shall be a reward.” (Protestants) — Thou shalt enjoy old age, or have posterity. (Calmet)"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Fall into smaller sins, (St. Gregory vi. in 2 Reg. xv. &c.) or into disgrace, as yippol (Haydock) rather intimates. (Vatable) (St. Augustine, City of God xi. 31.) — Both significations agree with the context. See Job v. 27., and Matthew xviii. 21. (Calmet) — He who is not subject to mortal sin, may still be exposed to many failings, and venial sins, which do not deprive him of the title of just; whereas the wicked consents to mortal sin, from which he riseth not so easily. Hence the wise man admonishes us not to lie in wait, or calumniously seek impiety in the house or soul of the just. (Worthington)"],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","From. To punish thee. (Calmet) — Thus will thy thirst of vengeance be disappointed. (Haydock) — The Hebrews believed that there was no evil, which was not caused by sin; and this was true in some sense. But still God often afflicts his servants, (ver. 16,) as the whole book of Job tends to prove. (Calmet)"],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Contend. Or Hebrew, “associate.” — Like. Ver. 1., and Psalm xxxvi. 1."],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","Come. Protestants, “no reward,” (Haydock) posterity, &c., as designated also by the lamp, ver. 14. (Calmet)"],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Detractors. Or those who speak ill of God or the king. (Calmet) — Protestants, “that are given to change,” (Haydock) and relapses."],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","These. Septuagint have an addition, and then our chap. xxx. to ver. 15., after which follows the remainder of this chapter and the ten first verses of the 31st. [chapter.] (Haydock) — Solomon here resumes the sententious style, chap. xxii. 17. (Calmet)"],["haydock",26,null,"haydock","Lips. And be deemed a friend. (Menochius)"],["haydock",27,null,"haydock","House, and support thy family. Before building, great deliberation is requisite, Luke xiv. 28. Those who attempt to instruct others, must first set good example."],["haydock",28,null,"haydock","Cause, and necessity. Septuagint, “be not a false witness against thy fellow-citizen.”"],["haydock",29,null,"haydock","Work. Revenge is often reprobated, though the law allowed of retaliation, which the more virtuous did not insist upon."],["haydock",30,null,"haydock","Man. Those who neglected their land were despised. (Calmet) — The ancient Romans esteemed agriculture as a most laudible and profitable employment. (Cato, Rust. i.; Cicero, Off. i.)"],["haydock",32,null,"haydock","Which. Septuagint, “at last I repented: I looked forward to receive instruction.”"],["haydock",33,null,"haydock","Said I, is not in Hebrew, chap. vi. 10. (Calmet) — Septuagint, “I will slumber a while,” to rest. Septuagint, “I will enfold my breast in my hands a little.” (Haydock)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}